# What `docker compose build` sends to the builder: this repository's source and nothing else.
#
# Docker: "You can use a `.dockerignore` file to exclude files or directories from the build context.
# This helps avoid sending unwanted files and directories to the builder" (Build context, docs.docker.com,
# accessed 2026-10-20). The build stage runs `COPY . .`, so anything left in the context reaches a
# layer of the build image, and a secret in a layer persists like one passed as a build argument:
# "Build arguments and environment variables are inappropriate for passing secrets to your build,
# because they persist in the final image" (Build secrets, docs.docker.com, accessed 2026-10-20).
#
# The Dockerfile reads go.mod, go.sum, the Go source, atlas.hcl, schema.hcl, migrations/,
# scripts/login-roles.sql and deploy/dev/migrate.sh. None of them is below.

# Sibling checkouts the workflows place in this workspace: deploy-dev checks out identity-kernel into
# .kernel/ and identity-control into .identity/. The kernel's deploy/dev/keys/signing holds its realm
# keystore, readable by its owner alone, and the builder could not even read it.
.kernel/
.identity/
.systemproof/
foundation-reference/

# Private keys and credentials, wherever a stack keeps them: a deploy directory's keys/, its .env, a
# server's Compose override, and a local token.
**/keys/
**/*.pem
**/.env
**/compose.override.yaml
.token

# Version control and local output: never read by the build.
.git
.github/
.image-scan/
.atlas
coverage
cov-src
*.out
